+# Specialty Filtering Is an Uncached Repository Predicate with Case Folding in the Query
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Both veterinarian list surfaces gain an optional specialty filter under `REQ-VET-003`. Three choices had to settle before implementation: where the predicate lives, how case-insensitive matching is reached across three database vendors, and whether the filtered read joins the `vets` cache.
+
+The vet package holds the project's only cached repository. It is also the only feature package whose two surfaces — the paged HTML page and the machine-readable document — read the same collection through different shapes.
+
+Owner search is the nearest precedent. It pushes its predicate into `OwnerRepository` and leans on the stored column's case handling. That second half is a recorded defect: PostgreSQL stores owner last names case-sensitively, so the same search returns different results per vendor.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Filter the cached collection in the controller and page it in Java.** Rejected: it adds a rule to a web controller, which the pattern catalog in `architecture-principles.md` bars, and hand-rolls pagination arithmetic the store already provides.
+2. **Repository predicate relying on each vendor's collation for case.** Rejected: it reproduces the owner-search defect. `specialties.name` is a case-sensitive type under H2 and PostgreSQL, and case-insensitive under MySQL, so results would diverge by vendor.
+3. **Repository predicate with case folding expressed in the query, filtered read uncached** (chosen).
+4. **Repository predicate with the filtered read cached under the specialty name.** Rejected: the `vets` cache declares neither eviction nor a size bound, so caller-supplied cache keys would grow it without limit.
+
+## Decision
+
+The predicate lives in `VetRepository`, as owner search puts its predicate in `OwnerRepository`. One predicate serves both surfaces: the HTML surface pages the narrowed list, and the machine-readable surface returns it unpaged. Paging over the narrowed list therefore reports counts the store computed, not counts derived in Java.
+
+Case folding is expressed in the query, so the match does not depend on any column's collation. H2 stores specialty names case-sensitively, so the existing H2-backed repository test can witness a regression here. Owner search had no such witness, which is why its vendor divergence went unseen.
+
+The specialty-filtered read is not cached. Unfiltered veterinarian reads keep the caching they have today, so a request without the parameter behaves exactly as before.
+
+Trimming the request value, and treating a blank one as no filter, is request adaptation and stays in the controller beside the owner-search precedent. A value with surrounding spaces therefore matches the trimmed name, settling that PRD open question.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Page totals on the narrowed page match the narrowed list, because the store computes them.
+- No caller-supplied value becomes a cache key. An arbitrary specialty costs one query and leaves no retained state.
+- The filtered read reaches the database on every request. The unfiltered list keeps its cache hit.
+- Whole-name matching means a caller needs the exact specialty name. NG-9 keeps free-text search out, and NG-10 keeps a page control out, so no surface offers the names yet.
+- The vet cache's missing eviction and size bound stay an open question. This decision routes around them rather than settling them.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003, REQ-VET-004
+
+- [`system-design.md` § Veterinarian list reads](../system-design.md#veterinarian-list-reads) — the rules this decision puts in force.
+- [Script-Managed Schema Across Three Database Vendors](2026-07-31-script-managed-multi-vendor-schema.md) — why per-vendor case behavior differs at all.
+- [Narrowing the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope](2026-08-11-non-goal-vet-specialty-filter.md) — the product boundary this read path serves.
